---
import AppLayout from '../../../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import { chatLikeGenZ, appPath, studio, supportLink } from '../../../../data/apps';

const path = appPath(chatLikeGenZ);
const sections = [
  ['conversions', 'Messages and conversion records'],
  ['device', 'Information on your device'],
  ['account', 'Accounts, wallet and recovery'],
  ['ads', 'Rewarded ads and privacy choices'],
  ['purchases', 'Google Play test purchases'],
  ['sharing', 'Purposes and service providers'],
  ['retention', 'Retention and deletion'],
  ['security', 'Security and international processing'],
  ['children', 'Children and changes'],
];
---

<AppLayout
  title="Chat Like Gen Z Privacy Policy | AppCrazy Labs"
  description="How Chat Like Gen Z handles conversion text, Gemini processing, Firebase wallet records, Google sign-in, test ads and purchases, retention and deletion requests."
  canonicalPath={`${path}privacy/`}
  heading="Chat Like Gen Z privacy policy"
  kicker="AppCrazy Labs · Privacy"
  backLabel={chatLikeGenZ.name}
  backHref={path}
>
  <div slot="meta" class="note-meta">
    <span>Effective: <time datetime="2026-10-06">6 October 2026</time></span>
    <span>Last updated: <time datetime="2026-10-06">6 October 2026</time></span>
  </div>

  <p class="archive-disclaimer">
    <strong>App:</strong> Chat Like Gen Z, an AppCrazy Labs product<br />
    <strong>Legal developer / publisher:</strong> Sidhanshu Udawat<br />
    <strong>Privacy contact:</strong> Sidhanshu Udawat - <a href={`mailto:${studio.email}`}>{studio.email}</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    This policy describes the current Android internal-test version of Chat Like Gen Z.
    In this policy, “we” means the developer named above. The app is not publicly
    available on Google Play yet. Advertising and purchases are test-only in this
    version. Confirmed in-app deletion is available in internal build 8 and later. We will update this
    page before broader release if the app or its data practices change.
  </p>
  <aside class="note-callout" aria-label="Privacy summary">
    <strong>Your words are processed remotely</strong>
    <p>
      Android conversions send the text you submit to a Firebase backend and Google
      Gemini. Encode also sends the writing preferences you choose. A generated result
      and conversion metadata are stored with your wallet record. Do not enter private
      information that you would not want processed by these services.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <nav class="policy-contents" aria-label="Privacy policy contents">
    <strong>In this policy</strong>
    <ol>{sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
  </nav>

  <section id="conversions" aria-labelledby="conversions-title">
    <h2 id="conversions-title">1. Messages and conversion records</h2>
    <p>
      When you request an encode or decode, the text you enter goes through an
      authenticated Firebase Cloud Function to Google Gemini for a generated answer.
      For encode, the request can also include your chosen writing style and optional
      age range, identity and pronouns. Decode does not send those preferences.
      Google processes the request to produce the answer. AI answers can be inaccurate.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Firebase wallet stores a conversion operation under your account identifier.
      Its record contains the request ID, status, timing information, a fingerprint
      derived from the submitted text and preferences, and the generated result on
      success. The current operation record does not save the plaintext input, but
      the input is transmitted to the backend and AI service. The fingerprint helps
      prevent a request ID being reused for different content. We do not claim that
      conversion text is processed only on your device or that no message data is stored.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="device" aria-labelledby="device-title">
    <h2 id="device-title">2. Information on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The app saves your writing profile locally, including any age range,
      identity, pronouns and style you select. It also uses local storage for
      a pending conversion request and the most recent completed input and result,
      so an interrupted request can be recovered without charging twice. A pending
      request can include your message and encode preferences. Reward-test state
      may also be kept locally while a reward is being checked.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clearing the app’s storage or uninstalling it removes app-managed local data
      on that device. Text you copy or share goes to the destination you choose;
      that destination’s privacy practices then apply. Device backups may retain
      copies under your device or backup-provider settings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="account" aria-labelledby="account-title">
    <h2 id="account-title">3. Accounts, wallet and recovery</h2>
    <p>
      You can start without Google sign-in. Firebase Authentication creates an
      anonymous account identifier, shown in the app as a Support ID, so a remote
      wallet can track your conversion balance. A new guest wallet starts with 10
      conversions; successful new conversions use one, while failed or cancelled
      requests return their reserved credit. Uninstalling an unlinked guest account
      can mean losing access to its balance, but does not itself delete server data.
    </p>
    <p>
      You may link a Google account to preserve or recover a wallet. Firebase
      Authentication then receives account details such as your Google identifier,
      email and basic profile information. If that Google account already has a
      wallet, restoring it switches to that wallet; balances are not combined.
      Google sign-in is required before test-pack checkout, not for free conversions.
      Firebase App Check, using Android integrity signals, helps protect backend
      requests from abuse. It does not make submitted text end-to-end encrypted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ads" aria-labelledby="ads-title">
    <h2 id="ads-title">4. Rewarded ads and privacy choices</h2>
    <p>
      The test build offers optional Google AdMob rewarded ads. A verified completed
      ad is intended to add three conversions after our server checks Google’s signed
      confirmation. The app also has demo ads that add no credits. Owned-unit ad
      completion and the full consent flow have not yet been verified on the test phone;
      live public ads are disabled.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google’s Mobile Ads SDK and User Messaging Platform (UMP) can process IP address,
      device or advertising identifiers, approximate location inferred from IP,
      ad interactions and diagnostics for ad delivery, measurement, security and
      consent management. The test configuration requests non-personalized, G-rated
      ads and applies under-age treatment to selected age groups. It does not request
      precise location. The app requests consent where required before loading ads
      and offers Settings → Ad privacy choices when a privacy-options form is required
      and available. A Console consent message still needs configuration and testing.
      See <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google’s Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="purchases" aria-labelledby="purchases-title">
    <h2 id="purchases-title">5. Google Play test purchases</h2>
    <p>
      Google Play test billing offers consumable packs of 100, 200 or 300 conversions
      to approved internal testers. There are no public live sales in this version.
      The app sends product and purchase identifiers to our backend, which checks
      the purchase and account association with Google Play before adding credits.
      We store wallet balances, grant records and billing-account mappings to
      reconcile purchases and avoid duplicate credits. Google Play handles payment;
      AppCrazy Labs does not receive your full payment-card details. Google controls
      its own purchase history. No subscription, lifetime Pro or RevenueCat service
      is used for this app.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sharing" aria-labelledby="sharing-title">
    <h2 id="sharing-title">6. Purposes and service providers</h2>
    <p>
      We use the information above to produce conversions, personalize encodes,
      recover results and balances, verify credits and test purchases, prevent misuse,
      respond to support and comply with legal obligations. Relevant providers are
      Google Firebase (Authentication, Cloud Functions, Firestore and App Check),
      Google Gemini through Vertex AI, Google AdMob and UMP for test ads, Google Play
      for test billing, and our email provider when you contact support. We may also
      disclose information when legally required. We do not use a separate analytics
      or crash-reporting SDK in the inspected test build.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you contact us, we receive your email address, message and any attachments
      you choose to send. Please do not email passwords, payment-card details or
      sensitive message text. See also Google’s <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">privacy information</a>
      {' '}for its services.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="retention" aria-labelledby="retention-title">
    <h2 id="retention-title">7. Retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Server conversion results and the fingerprint derived from submitted content expire
      seven days after creation. The backend stops returning expired results and removes
      them on access. An hourly cleanup job also removes expired results and fingerprints;
      physical cleanup can be delayed by retries, outages or a processing backlog. This is
      not a promise that every physical copy disappears exactly at seven days. Active
      requests are allowed to settle or time out safely before cleanup. Minimal request IDs,
      status and timing metadata remain with the wallet until account deletion to prevent
      old retries from spending a conversion again. This mechanism does not use Firestore
      TTL to delete entire conversion records.
    </p>
    <p>
      Firebase account, wallet, linked Google identity, billing mappings and active-account
      credit records remain while the account exists. Expired reward tickets are cleaned
      after an additional day for reconciliation. Your latest input/result and writing
      profile can remain on your device until replaced or cleared; the server's seven-day
      rule does not erase local copies, screenshots, shared text or device backups.
    </p>
    <p>
      In internal build 8 and later, use <strong>Settings → Delete account &amp; data</strong>.
      Confirm Google sign-in again if linked, then confirm permanent deletion. The server
      closes the account immediately, blocks new wallet writes, removes the Firebase Auth
      account and Google link, wallet, conversion records, billing mappings and reward
      tickets. Interrupted cleanup retries hourly. This also removes unused purchased
      conversions and does not request a Google Play refund. The Google account itself is
      not deleted. The app clears app-managed personal data on the requesting phone and
      attempts to revoke its Google sign-in access. Other/offline devices and backups may
      need local cleanup or removal through their own settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      After deletion, transaction-token digests with pack/reward type, amount, timing and
      reversal status, but without your Firebase account ID, email, Google identity or
      messages, remain for 180 days to prevent duplicate credits and reconcile refunds.
      A hashed account-deletion receipt remains for 90 days to block stale requests and
      record completion. Hourly cleanup removes these records after their retention
      deadlines, subject to the same operational delays. These are the current test app's
      operational retention periods, not a claim that a law requires this duration.
      Google Play controls its own purchase history. The configured Cloud Logging buckets
      retain default operational logs for 30 days and required audit logs for 400 days; the
      required-audit bucket is locked. These platform logs are separate from app wallet data
      and are not erased by account deletion. Data held by Google, Gemini, AdMob and our
      email provider follow their separate retention practices.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also request deletion without signing in through the
      <a href={`${path}data-deletion/`}>Chat Like Gen Z data-deletion page</a>.
      Support verifies ownership before processing; knowing a Support ID alone is not enough.
      Email requests are reviewed manually and sending one does not immediately delete an
      account. Support correspondence is separate from the automated account cleanup; ask
      for its deletion too if desired. We will explain any specific legal hold before retaining
      requested correspondence. No fixed support completion time is promised.
    </p>
    <p>
      Depending on your location, you may have rights to access, correct or delete
      personal information, or object to or restrict processing. Email{' '}
      <a href={supportLink('Chat Like Gen Z privacy request')}>{studio.email}</a>
      {' '}with your Support ID if you have it. We may ask for limited information
      to verify ownership before acting on a request.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security" aria-labelledby="security-title">
    <h2 id="security-title">8. Security and international processing</h2>
    <p>
      Backend requests use encrypted connections, Firebase Authentication and App
      Check; the app cannot directly edit the server wallet. This limits some risks
      but does not guarantee absolute security. Submitted text is visible to the
      backend and AI service for processing, so it is not end-to-end encrypted.
      Google services and our email provider may process information outside your
      country, including the United States, subject to their policies and applicable
      safeguards.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="children" aria-labelledby="children-title">
    <h2 id="children-title">9. Children and changes</h2>
    <p>
      Chat Like Gen Z is not directed at children under 13. Its profile includes
      a 13–17 age range, so some users may be teenagers. If you believe a child
      under 13 has supplied personal information, contact us so we can investigate.
    </p>
    <p>
      We may revise this policy when the app, providers or test configuration change.
      The new version will appear here with an updated date. For privacy questions,
      contact Sidhanshu Udawat at{' '}
      <a href={supportLink('Chat Like Gen Z privacy enquiry')}>{studio.email}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</AppLayout>
